/cross-area-composition.test.ts` 与 `cross-area/cross-area-facades.ts` 引用；requiredTests 与 coverage 守卫同步。\" — Hmm, \"移除树中不存在的...引用\" - this must refer to the a6 spec (A06) which still references them. Since targetScope includes only a5 spec... but it also includes the new reverify spec.\n\nHmm, hold on. Maybe the sequence is: the reference solution author looked at the tree at base commit, saw a5 spec references cross-area-composition.test.ts + cross-area-facades.ts which did NOT exist → so they implemented them (created the files), thus resolving the drift, rather than deleting the references. And this acceptance criterion is a leftover from a template (like A02's \"removed the stale references\")... \n\nHmm, actually no. Look: the A02 case removed the references because there was no test. For A05, the criteria 3 says implement them. So they're contradictory in a subtle way: maybe A05's spec referenced them and the solution CHOSE to implement (creating real files) — for A2 the solution chose to remove. Hmm, but the acceptance criterion explicitly says \"remove the references that don't exist in the tree\".\n\nOK here's another interpretation: The criterion means \"for any references in the tree that point to files that do not exist, remove them\" — i.e., after my work, ensure the spec's requiredTests all point at existing files. Since I'm creating cross-area-composition.test.ts and cross-area-facades.ts, those references become valid. But maybe there are OTHER non-existent references, e.g. `artifacts/context-compression-p5/**` legacy path patterns.\n\nHmm. Let me just check A06 spec and the a5 spec for references and see which files don't exist.\n\nGiven ambiguity, best approach: make everything referenced by the A5 spec exist. Create cross-area facades + composition test + a5-deep + coverage guard. Restore the 8 deleted tests. Update specs so requiredTests all point at existing files. Write docs/acceptance/SHA256SUMS. That satisfies both readings as much as possible