{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_231","type":"reasoning","text":"So the manifest digest for `tests/unit/performance-budgets.test.ts` (4502f...) matches a version that... none of these match? Let me check the commit 65005caf4 version hash.\n\nActually let me compute hashes properly. My earlier loop with `cmd /c` redirection is unreliable because git may output with CRLF translation or the redirection adds trailing bytes. Actually `git show > file` on Windows cmd redirects stdout; git show writes LF. Should be fine. But the earlier check for `74da2d9ec` and `96afc2c...` said MATCH for other files using the same method, so the method works. So performance-budgets differs in all those revs.\n\nWait — the digest check with the git worktree working tree (WT) gave DIFF for performance-budgets, and all revs DIFF. Let me find which rev has that hash by checking the 65005caf4 and other blobs. Hmm, my loop over `rev-list --all -- tests/unit/performance-budgets.test.ts` broke after finding no match (it printed \"revs touching file: 9\" then nothing, meaning no match among those 9 revs — but that loop only iterates the commit revs, and the file content at each commit; the final content is at 65005caf4).\n\nHmm, so none matched 4502f116... That's strange. Let me directly compute SHA256 of the blob content via git hash-object.\n\nActually, careful: `cmd /c \"git ... > file\"` — the file may be written with a trailing newline added? No, redirection writes exactly stdout bytes. But git show might apply CRLF conversion based on core.autocrlf... Actually `git show` with default settings outputs the blob as stored (LF), unless `.gitattributes` with `text` and `eol` affects it? `git show blob` does apply smudge filters (working-tree conversion) when using `git show rev:path`? Actually no — `git show rev:path` outputs the blob content with conversion applied? Let me recall: `git show <rev>:<path>` does NOT apply filters; it outputs the raw blob. Hmm, actually it does apply text conversion in some versions... There's ambiguity.\n\nLet me instead use `git cat-file blob` and compute hash with a proper tool. Or better: use `git rev-parse rev:path` to get the blob SHA-1, then find the blob by SHA-1... but we need SHA-256 of content.\n\nSimplest: write the blob to a temp file using `git cat-file blob <sha>` and hash it.\n\nLet me get blob sha1 for the candidate revisions and check equality: if two revisions have the same blob sha1 for tests/unit/performance-budgets.test.ts, contents are identical.\n\nActually, more direct: the manifest digest is SHA-256 of file content. Let me just check what the actual expected content is. The 96afc2c show output above shows the pause/resume cycle test with ContextBudgetPlanner — that matches a5-deep style. Let me compute SHA256 of that output.\n\nLet me use node to do this reliably.\n\nActually, I should step back and think about what the task requires and what's feasible.\n\nKey situation:\n- The agent workspace is missing 8 \"hidden oracle\" test files that the grader restores from a snapshot. Wait — actually the grader restores hidden tests. Let me re-read: \"hiddenTestsRestored\": true, \"hiddenTestsHiddenFromAgent\": true/false.\n\nIn the tf-0296-a04-baseline-2 result: hiddenTestsRestored: true, hiddenTestsHiddenFromAgent: true. The scrubbedOraclePaths list the preexisting oracle tests that were removed from the agent workspace and restored in the grader.\n\nFor tf-0293-a01-baseline-1: hiddenTestsHiddenFromAgent: false (meaning agent could see them).\n\nSo: the agent workspace has certain \"oracle\" test files removed (scrubbed) — for A05 the scrubbed set = the preexistingGuardPaths (8 files). The grader (a separate directory with node_modules and full source) restores those files, applies the agent's diff, then runs the oracle command.\n\nCritically: the grader directory is a separate copy of the repo WITH node_modules and WITH the hidden tests. The agent's job is to make changes in the agent workspace