{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_308","type":"reasoning","text":"The grader restores hidden tests but the production tree stays as the agent left it. So the hidden oracle for tf-0297-a05 includes:\n- tests/unit/resource-aware-wave-scheduler.test.ts (preexisting guard, restored)\n- tests/unit/performance-budgets.test.ts (preexisting guard, restored)\n- tests/unit/deprecation-counters.test.ts (preexisting guard, restored)\n- openclaw_runtime/src/observability/deprecation-counters.test.ts (restored)\n- openclaw_runtime/src/agents/agent-hooks/compaction-budget-shadow.test.ts (restored)\n- openclaw_runtime/packages/agent-core/src/harness/compaction/compression-planner.test.ts (restored)\n- openclaw_runtime/src/context-engine/context-orchestrator.test.ts (restored)\n- openclaw_runtime/src/agents/agent-hooks/compaction-promotion.test.ts (restored)\n- tests/unit/a05-a5-gate-coverage.test.ts (hidden signal — the agent must create this? No wait...)\n\nHmm. Let's re-read. `hiddenSignalPaths: tests/unit/a05-a5-gate-coverage.test.ts` and `preexistingGuardPaths` excludes a05-a5-gate-coverage.test.ts. Since the a05 coverage test is NOT in preexistingGuardPaths, and it IS in hiddenSignalPaths, and testPaths includes it... \n\nLooking at the A01 result: `oracleWrites: [\"tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts\"]` and `preexistingOraclePaths` included a01 coverage test. Hmm, for A01, the a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts existed in the agent workspace (it's a preexisting file, since base has it? No—agent had a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts present because manifest for tf-0293-a01 shows preexistingGuardPaths didn't include it, but hiddenSignalPaths did).\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. In tf-0293-a01 baseline-1 result:\n- `oracleWrites: [\"tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts\"]` — the oracle wrote this file (restored it).\n- `scrubbedOraclePaths` = preexistingOraclePaths = the 6 guard paths, not including a01 coverage.\n- hmm, but `oracleWrites` includes a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts. \n\nHmm, this is confusing. Let me look at the manifest for tf-0293-a01 again: testPaths included a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts, hiddenSignalPaths = [a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts], preexistingGuardPaths = the 6 others.\n\nSo `hiddenSignalPaths` = files whose content is a hidden signal — meaning the AGENT must create them (they're the \"solution\" file the agent produces and the grader replaces/verifies). Wait but then \"oracleWrites\" means the oracle wrote/restored that path — meaning the grader restores the canonical version from the reference solution, overwriting whatever the agent produced. That's how \"deterministic-final-state\" graders work: they restore the reference version of the hidden signal file and run.\n\nHmm, but that would make the agent's job just to produce the other artifacts. Actually let me check: in the A01 result, agent had a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts (agent's own version, quite different/longer — I saw it above, it's a rich test file). The grader's version is the short one. So yes, the grader overrode the agent's file with the reference version.\n\nSo for A05, the grader will restore `tests/unit/a05-a5-gate-coverage.test.ts` from the reference commit, PLUS all 8 preexisting guard test files. Then it runs:\n- `pnpm exec vitest run tests/unit/resource-aware-wave-scheduler.test.ts tests/unit/performance-budgets.test.ts tests/unit/deprecation-counters.test.ts tests/unit/a05-a5-gate-coverage.test.ts --maxWorkers=1`\n- `pnpm --dir openclaw_runtime exec node scripts/run-vitest.mjs run src/observability/deprecation-counters.test.ts src/agents/agent-hooks/compaction-budget-shadow.test.ts packages/agent-core/src/harness/compaction/compression-planner.test.ts src/context-engine/context-orchestrator.test.ts src/agents/agent-hooks/compaction-promotion.test.ts --maxWorkers=1`\n\nWait but the second command includes tests/unit/a05-a5-gate-coverage.test.ts? No — it includes root tests, but the second command is in openclaw_runtime. Actually let's just focus: the oracle commands reference:\nRoot command files: resource-aware-wave-scheduler, performance-budgets, deprecation-counters, a05-a5-gate-coverage.\nRuntime command files: deprecation-counters.test.ts, compaction-budget-shadow.test.ts, compression-planner.test.ts, context-orchestrator.test.ts, compaction-promotion.test.ts.\n\nNote the runtime env is sensitive: the runtime tests run under openclaw_runtime's own vitest config (`scripts/run-vitest.mjs`). Note also that `performance-budgets.test.ts` (reference version) imports ContextBudgetPlanner/ResumeContractVerifier/FingerprintLeaseCoordinator from `../../openclaw_runtime/src/agents/cross-area/cross-area-facades.js`. So it will fail if cross-area-facades.ts doesn't exist. That's the core \"judgment 3\" implementation.\n\nAlso the runtime tests: `context-orchestrator.test.ts` at 74da2d9ec differs from base (fd04f0b4). But the runtime oracle command only runs contexts tests at the preexisting version (40b9500b...) — which matched 74da2d9ec and 96afc2c. My earlier check found `openclaw_runtime/src/context-engine/context-orchestrator.test.ts => 74da2d9ec,96afc2c` MATCH. So the pinned runtime context-orchestrator.test.ts = 74da2d9ec version, which differs from base fd04f0b4 version.\n\nHmm, that's important! The base fd04f0b4 has context-orchestrator.ts untracked by the change... wait, the diff fd04→74da for context-orchestrator.ts had changes (recordContextFinalSnapshot). And context-orchestrator.test.ts at fd04 is 65ffc0b9f, at 74da is 42cd8e228. So the runtime oracle requires the *newer* context-orchestrator.test.ts (74da version), which itself requires the *newer* context-orchestrator.ts (with recordContextFinalSnapshot, a new file context-final-snapshot.ts).\n\nBut wait — the workspace's context-orchestrator.ts matches fd04 (SAME as base). So running the pinned runtime context-orchestrator.test.ts against the workspace's fd04 context-orchestrator.ts... would it pass? Let me check whether the 74da context-orchestrator.test.ts imports context-final-snapshot.\n\nHmm, this is getting deep. Let me actually just check: does the pinned context-orchestrator.test.ts (40b9500b) differ from the base one? Let me diff base version vs pinned version.\n\nActually my digest check earlier said:\n- openclaw_runtime/src/context-engine/context-orchestrator.test.ts => MATCH at 74da2d9ec and 96afc2c (not at fd04f0b4).\n\nSo pinned != base version. Let me diff them.\n\nActually, the crucial realization: this environment reproduces a historical trial. The base commit is fd04f0b4. The reference commit (which the manifest cites as the \"reference solution\") is 47388bf3, whose acceptance record is the same file as at 74da2d9ec (4cec9973...). But the *tests* pinned in inputDigests match 74da2d9ec (the corpus/HEAD resealed state), which is a descendant of 96afc2c7.\n\nWait: is 74da2d9ec a descendant of the reference commit 47388bf3? Earlier: `merge-base --is-ancestor 47388 fd04` → 1 (not ancestor). And fd04 is the *base* for this trial but the git worktree HEAD is 74da2d9ec (a different lineage). The manifest's `source.candidate` = 96afc2c7.\n\nSo the workspace base (fd04f0b4) is the base commit; the corpus candidate is 96afc2c7; and hidden tests come from the corpus at that candidate. The reference solution is 47388bf3 which is the A05 commit on top of fd04.\n\nBut pinned digests match 74da2d9ec for many files... 74da is HEADEADS. Hmm, the manifest was created from the resealed corpus, and the test files are \"preexisting\" files whose content at the time of manifest creation matched 74da2d9ec. Let me verify: is 74da2d9ec an ancestor of 96afc2c7, or vice versa?\n\nLet me check whether 96afc2c7's tree equals 74da's tree for these files. Earlier both matched for:\n- a05 rev spec: 74da, 96afc2c, 65005\n- resource-aware-wave-scheduler.test.ts: 74da, 96afc2c, 65005, 47388, fd04 — so this preexisting file is identical everywhere. Good.\n- deprecation-counters.test.ts: same set.\n- observability/deprecation-counters.test.ts: same set.\n- compaction-budget-shadow.test.ts: same set.\n- compression-planner.test.ts: same set.\n- context-orchestrator.test.ts: only 74da and 96afc2c → so this file was *changed* between fd04 (base) and the candidate! Not in 47388?? wait 47388 doesn't have it matching, and fd04 doesn't. So the candidate 96afc2c changed context-orchestrator.test.ts relative to base fd04.\n\nHmm, that means the hidden runtime context-orchestrator.test.ts is the *newer* one that expects `recordContextFinalSnapshot`. But the base workspace has the *older* context-orchestrator.ts. So the grader would restore the new test file and it would fail against the old source... \n\nHold on. That can't be right for a fair task. Let me re-examine: maybe the manifest's base commit for trial tf-0297-a05 is fd04f0b4, but the *grader* reconstructs a full \"candidate\" workspace = base + agent diff, and the hidden test files come from the candidate 96afc2c7 tree.\n\nHmm wait, but the completed A01 trial shows grader's context-orchestrator.test.ts = a01base (72ba3c32) version. That's because for A01, the relevant files matched the a01 base. Let me check what a01's base is: `baseCommit: 72ba3c32e31cf071aa98c3ed335f76d9b12ef1c1`. Hmm, and the A01 task's manifest oracle didn't include context-orchestrator.test.ts.\n\nOK. I think I'm overcomplicating. Let me directly check: does the workspace's context-orchestrator.test.ts (in agent) match fd04? Earlier cmp.cjs said agent's context-orchestrator.ts matches fd04. I didn't test the .test.ts. Let me check what the agent workspace contains for context-orchestrator.test.ts — it may be absent (it's a hidden preexisting guard! indeed `openclaw_runtime/src/observability/deprecation-counters.test.ts` was absent from agent). Actually the diff between base and agent showed these 8 files missing from agent:\n- compression-planner.test.ts\n- compaction-budget-shadow.test.ts\n- compaction-promotion.test.ts\n- context-orchestrator.test.ts\n- observability/deprecation-counters.test.ts\n- tests/unit/deprecation-counters.test.ts\n- tests/unit/performance-budgets.test.ts\n- tests/unit/resource-aware-wave-scheduler.test.ts\n\nSo the agent workspace has these hidden guard files REMOVED (scrubbed). The base/ sibling has them (at fd04 content). The grader restores them from the pinned candidate content.\n\nSo: for context-orchestrator.test.ts, base(fd04) content vs pinned content differ. The grader will restore the pinned (candidate) version. Will it pass against the workspace's context-orchestrator.ts (fd04 version)? \n\nLet me diff the pinned context-orchestrator.test.ts (74da/96afc2c) against fd04's version. From the diff I printed above (fd04→74da for context-orchestrator.test.ts), the changes were about VToken retirement (\"TF-0496 retired VToken\") and native capability. Those tests reference `lineage.nativeContext` etc. Hmm, and the source change (context-orchestrator.ts fd04→74da) added `recordContextFinalSnapshot`. But the *test* changes were about vtoken retirement.\n\nWait — the fd04→74da diff includes many unrelated commits (fd04 is the base of A05, but 74da is HEAD of a much later lineage). So the fd04→74da diff is huge (245 files). The test file context-orchestrator.test.ts changed due to VToken retirement work, not due to A05.\n\nBut: is the workspace based on fd04 or on 96afc2c7? cmp.cjs said agent's context-orchestrator.ts SAME as fd04 and DIFF from 96afc2c7. So workspace = fd04 lineage.\n\nYet the pinned context-orchestrator.test.ts matches 74da/96afc2c (the later lineage) and NOT fd04. That's contradictory: if the workspace is fd04-based, restoring a late-lineage test file that expects retired-VToken behavior would fail against fd04 source.\n\nUnless... 96afc2c7's context-orchestrator.test.ts content happens to also be what fd04+A05-change would produce? No, A05 doesn't touch it.\n\nHmm, let me just directly compare: is the workspace (agent) context-orchestrator.ts the same as 96afc2c7's